arguments.prompt:
You are working in /tmp/sv-3ee5a84b8ed54bd58f7dd3b9fd617943/UniversityCourseCatalog — a .NET 8 Blazor Web App created with `dotnet new blazor --interactivity Server`.

Build a university course catalog application with the following requirements:
- Browse departments and courses in a searchable, filterable list
- View course detail pages (description, schedule, credits, instructor bio)
- Students submit an enrollment request form (name, student ID, course selection) — standard HTML form, no wizards or real-time validation
- Homepage shows featured courses and department links
- No authentication — open to all students and visitors
- Hosted on university intranet with fast, reliable connectivity

The project already has the standard Blazor template files. Here's what you need to do:

## 1. Create Models

Create file `Models/Department.cs`:
```csharp
namespace UniversityCourseCatalog.Models;

public class Department
{
    public int Id { get; set; }
    public string Name { get; set; } = string.Empty;
    public string Code { get; set; } = string.Empty;
    public string Description { get; set; } = string.Empty;
}
```

Create file `Models/Course.cs`:
```csharp
namespace UniversityCourseCatalog.Models;

public class Course
{
    public int Id { get; set; }
    public string Title { get; set; } = string.Empty;
    public string Code { get; set; } = string.Empty;
    public string Description { get; set; } = string.Empty;
    public int Credits { get; set; }
    public string Schedule { get; set; } = string.Empty;
    public string InstructorName { get; set; } = string.Empty;
    public string InstructorBio { get; set; } = string.Empty;
    public int DepartmentId { get; set; }
    public string DepartmentName { get; set; } = string.Empty;
    public bool IsFeatured { get; set; }
}
```

Create file `Models/EnrollmentRequest.cs`:
```csharp
using System.ComponentModel.DataAnnotations;

namespace UniversityCourseCatalog.Models;

public class EnrollmentRequest
{
    [Required(ErrorMessage = "Name is required.")]
    [StringLength(100, ErrorMessage = "Name cannot exceed 100 characters.")]
    public string StudentName { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    [Required(ErrorMessage = "Student ID is required.")]
    [RegularExpression(@"^[A-Z]{2}\d{6}$", ErrorMessage = "Student ID must be in format: two uppercase letters followed by six digits (e.g., CS123456).")]
    public string StudentId { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    [Required(ErrorMessage = "Please select a course.")]
    [Range(1, int.MaxValue, ErrorMessage = "Please select a course.")]
    public int CourseId { get; set; }
}
```

## 2. Create Services

Create file `Services/ICatalogService.cs`:
```csharp
using UniversityCourseCatalog.Models;

namespace UniversityCourseCatalog.Services;

public interface ICatalogService
{
    List<Department> GetDepartments();
    Department? GetDepartment(int id);
    List<Course> GetCourses(string? searchTerm = null, int? departmentId = null);
    Course? GetCourse(int id);
    List<Course> GetFeaturedCourses();
    bool SubmitEnrollmentRequest(EnrollmentRequest request);
}
```

Create file `Services/CatalogService.cs` with an in-memory implementation that has sample data for at least 4 departments (Computer Science, Mathematics, Physics, English) and 3+ courses per department. Some courses should be marked as featured. Include realistic instructor bios and schedules.

## 3. Register the Service

Update `Program.cs` to add:
```csharp
using UniversityCourseCatalog.Services;
```
and before `var app = builder.Build();`:
```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<ICatalogService, CatalogService>();
```

## 4. Create Pages

### Update `Components/Pages/Home.razor`
Replace with a homepage that shows:
- A welcome header for "State University Course Catalog"
- A section of featured courses (cards with title, code, credits, and a link to details)
- A section listing departments with links to browse courses filtered by department

### Create `Components/Pages/Courses.razor`
Route: `/courses`
- Shows a search input and a department filter dropdown
- Lists matching courses in a table or card layout showing code, title, department, credits, schedule
- Each course links to its detail page
- Use interactive server rendering for the search/filter

### Create `Components/Pages/CourseDetail.razor`
Route: `/courses/{Id:int}`
- Shows full course details: title, code, department, description, credits, schedule
- Shows instructor name and bio
- Link back to course list

### Create `Components/Pages/Enroll.razor`
Route: `/enroll`
- Standard HTML form with EditForm
- Fields: Student Name, Student ID, Course Selection (dropdown of all courses)
- Uses DataAnnotationsValidator and ValidationSummary
- On successful submission, shows a confirmation message
- Use interactive server rendering for form handling

## 5. Update Navigation

Update `Components/Layout/NavMenu.razor` to include nav links for:
- Home (/)
- Courses (/courses)
- Enroll (/enroll)

Remove the Counter and Weather links.

## 6. Delete Unused Pages

Delete these files:
- Components/Pages/Counter.razor
- Components/Pages/Weather.razor

## 7. Update App.razor and MainLayout

In `Components/Layout/MainLayout.razor`, update the page title or any branding to say "University Course Catalog" instead of the default app name.

## 8. Build and verify

Run `dotnet build` at the end and make sure there are no errors.

Important implementation notes:
- The Courses page and Enroll page need `@rendermode InteractiveServer` since they have interactive elements (search/filter, form submission)
- Home and CourseDetail can use static SSR (no rendermode needed)
- Use `@inject ICatalogService CatalogService` to access the service
- Keep styling simple using the existing Bootstrap classes from the template

